IMPLEMENTATION OF VICTIM RECOVERY

Article 60

(1) The implementation of recovery activities for women and child victims includes:

a) Health services;

Health services are provided by health workers at health facilities owned by the government, regional government, and the community, including the private sector by providing medical treatment and health recovery services for victims.

b) Victim assistance;

Victim assistance is provided by health workers, social workers, volunteer assistants, and/or spiritual counselors by providing counseling, therapy, spiritual guidance and advocacy for the strengthening and recovery of victims.

c) Counseling;

Counseling is provided by social workers, volunteer assistants, by listening empathetically and exploring problems for the psychological strengthening of victims.

d) Spiritual guidance;

Spiritual guidance is provided by spiritual counselors by providing explanations regarding their rights and obligations, as well as strengthening faith and piety in accordance with the religion and beliefs they adhere to.

e) Resocialization.

Resocialization of victims is carried out by social agencies and social institutions so that victims can resume their social functions in society.

Article 9

Every person who hears, sees, or knows of the occurrence of domestic violence is obliged to make efforts in accordance with their capacity to:

a. prevent the continuation of criminal acts;

b. provide protection to victims;

c. provide emergency assistance; and

d. assist in the process of submitting an application for protection orders.

Article 35

(1) Specific protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 30 includes children in conflict with the law and children who are victims of criminal acts, which is the obligation and responsibility of the government and society.

(2) Specific protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through:

a. treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;

b. provision of special accompanying officers for children from an early stage;

c. provision of special facilities and infrastructure;

d. imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of the child;

e. continuous monitoring and recording of the development of children in conflict with the law;

f. provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; and

g. protection from identity disclosure through mass media and to avoid labeling.

(3) Specific protection for children who become victims of criminal acts as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through:

a. rehabilitation efforts, both in institutions and outside institutions;

b. protection efforts from identity disclosure through mass media and to avoid labeling;

c. provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses and expert witnesses, whether physical, mental, or social; and

d. provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments.

Part Two

Right to Life

Article 46

The right to life for Persons with Disabilities includes the right to:

a. Respect for integrity;

b. not be deprived of life;

c. receive care and upbringing that ensures the continuity of life;

d. freedom from neglect, shackling, confinement, and isolation;

e. freedom from threats and various forms of exploitation; and

f. freedom from torture, cruel, inhuman, and degrading treatment and punishment.

Part Three Right to Freedom from Stigma Article 47

The right to freedom from stigma for Persons with Disabilities includes the right to freedom from harassment, insult, and negative labeling related to their disability condition.

Part Four Right to Privacy Article 48

The right to privacy for Persons with Disabilities includes the right to:

a. be recognized as a human person who can demand and obtain equal treatment and Protection in accordance with human dignity in public;

b. form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage;

c. Respect for home and family;

d. obtain Protection for personal and family life; and

e. be protected in the confidentiality of personal data, correspondence, and other forms of personal communication, including health data and information.

Part Five

Right to Justice and Legal Protection

Article 49

The right to justice and legal protection for Persons with Disabilities includes the right to:

a. equal treatment before the law;

b. be recognized as a legal subject;

c. own and inherit movable or immovable property;

d. control financial matters or appoint a person to represent their interests in financial affairs;

e. obtain access to banking and non-banking services;

f. obtain the provision of Accessibility in judicial services;

g. Protection from all pressure, violence, persecution, discrimination, and/or deprivation or seizure of property rights;

h. choose and appoint a person to represent their interests in civil matters inside and outside the court; and

i. be protected in their intellectual property rights.

Part Six

Right to Education

Article 50

The right to education for Persons with Disabilities includes the right to:

a. obtain quality education in educational units of all types, pathways, and levels of education inclusively and specifically;

b. have Equal Opportunity to become an educator or educational personnel in educational units of all types, pathways, and levels of education;

c. have Equal Opportunity as a provider of quality education in educational units of all types, pathways, and levels of education; and

d. obtain Reasonable Accommodation as a student.

